N={
"fourth-of-july-cookout-menu":"""### Scaling the menu

Plan on about 1/3 pound of meat per adult when there are plenty of sides, and about 1/2 pound when meat is the star. Count on 1 1/2 burgers or hot dogs per guest, plus extra for teenagers. For sides, a half cup to a cup per person of each salad is plenty when you offer three or more. Buy a bag of ice per four to five guests on a hot day. Write the numbers down before you shop, and you will not run out of buns at the worst possible moment or end up with three pounds of leftover potato salad. When in doubt, extra hot dogs are the cheapest insurance in the cooler. Keep a second bag of charcoal on hand so a long, lazy afternoon of grilling never ends early.""",
"gifts-under-30-for-grillers":"""### A last word on value

Price is only half of value. A small gift earns its keep by how often it gets used. A thermometer may be pulled out every single cook for years, so it costs pennies per use. A tin of balm keeps a grill, a griddle and a skillet in good shape season after season. Homemade rubs disappear fast and invite the recipient to come back and ask for more, which is the best compliment a homemade gift can receive. When you are choosing between two small gifts, picture the recipient on an ordinary Tuesday night. The gift they reach for on that ordinary night is the one to buy. Add a note in your own handwriting, and even a nine-dollar tin feels like a thoughtful present.""",
"cast-iron-cornbread-and-chili-over-coals":"""### Make it a one-fire supper

Start the chili first over a full bed of coals in a Dutch oven or deep skillet, since it benefits from a long, gentle simmer. While it bubbles, mix the cornbread batter. When the chili is nearly done, move it to the cooler side, rake fresh coals under the other side, and bake the cornbread in a preheated, buttered skillet. Check the cornbread often by sight and with a toothpick; open-fire baking runs hotter at the bottom than an oven, so a ring of coals around the edge rather than directly under the center prevents scorching. Serve the chili ladled over wedges of warm cornbread, with shredded cheese, sliced scallions and sour cream on the side. One fire, two cast-iron pans and a hearty meal for a crowd on a cool evening.""",
"gifts-for-home-cooks":"""### The thank-you that keeps giving

When a cook receives a gift that changes how they cook, they tend to invite the giver to the first meal. That is the hidden bonus of practical cooking gifts: they come back to you as dinner. Ask the recipient what they plan to cook first, and offer to bring a side or a bottle of something. A grill in particular tends to become a gathering point, and the person who gave it becomes part of every story about the first cookout, the first perfectly seared steak and the first summer of backyard dinners. If you are unsure between two gifts, choose the one more likely to bring people to the same table. Practical gifts age well; a well-loved carbon steel grill can still be cooking dinner many years from now.""",
"marinades-that-work":"""### A quick-reference marinade matrix

Use this to improvise when you do not have a recipe handy:

| Base | Add | Goes with |
| Olive oil and lemon | Garlic, oregano | Chicken, fish, vegetables |
| Soy sauce and rice vinegar | Ginger, scallion, sesame | Beef, pork, tofu |
| Yogurt and lemon | Garam masala, garlic | Chicken, lamb |
| Orange and lime | Cumin, chipotle | Pork, shrimp |
| Balsamic and olive oil | Rosemary, Dijon | Steak, mushrooms |

Start with the base, add salt and aromatics, taste a drop on a piece of bread and adjust. A good marinade should taste a little too strong on its own, because much of it clings only to the surface.""",
"cooking-for-a-crowd-one-grill":"""### Menu choices that make one grill enough

Pick foods that cook in batches and hold well. Sausages, chicken thighs, and kebabs tolerate a little waiting on the cool side without drying out, while thin fish fillets and well-done burgers do not. A whole spatchcocked chicken occupies the cool side for most of an hour without attention, freeing the hot side for quick bites. Build at least half the menu from sides that need no grill at all. And when the crowd is truly huge, consider a flat-top: two griddle plates turn a Full Size Slot Grill into a continuous cooking surface for smash burgers, which take two or three minutes each and can be stacked onto toasted buns in an assembly line. Speed wins at big parties, and simple, fast food keeps the cook out of the weeds.""",
"fathers-day-gift-guide":"""### Gifts by dad personality

- **The perfectionist:** a fast, accurate thermometer and a notebook for recording cooks.
- **The adventurer:** a Compact grill for road trips, fishing trips and camping.
- **The breakfast king:** griddle plates for weekend pancakes, bacon and eggs.
- **The host:** a Full Size grill for backyard parties.
- **The tinkerer:** a set of homemade rubs to test and tweak.
- **The minimalist:** one great tool he will use for years rather than a drawer of gadgets.

Match the gift to how he actually spends his weekends, and it will feel personal even without engraving.""",
"two-zone-fire-explained":"""### Two-zone fire on a small grill

On a compact grill, the cool zone is small, so arrange coals tightly against one end and leave the other third or so bare. Turn food often and rotate it so the side facing the coals changes. Use the thermometer more frequently, since smaller grills can swing temperature quickly. On a grill without a lid, such as a Slot Grill, indirect cooking is gentler and slower than in a covered kettle, so thick cuts take longer. Plan for it, give yourself extra time, and cover large pieces loosely with an inverted foil pan or a sheet of foil if you need to trap a little heat. Small fires reward attention, but they also respond quickly when you adjust, which makes them wonderful teachers.""",
"chicken-souvlaki-skewers":"""### Building better skewers

Cut the chicken into even 1 1/4-inch pieces so everything cooks at the same rate. Thread them snugly but not crammed together; a small gap lets heat reach every side. If you use wooden skewers, soak them in water for 30 minutes first so they do not burn. Flat metal skewers stop the chunks from twirling every time you rotate them. Cook the vegetables on separate skewers from the meat, because onions and peppers cook at a different pace than chicken. Grill over direct heat, turning every couple of minutes, and check with a thermometer for 165°F in the thickest piece. Serve with warm pita charred briefly on the grates, a bowl of tzatziki and a squeeze of lemon over everything at the end.""",
"all-purpose-bbq-rubs":"""### Rub safety and allergies

When you share rubs, label common allergens. Some chili powders and spice blends include additives, and some people are sensitive to mustard, sesame or celery seed. Never use a rub shaker directly over raw meat and then over cooked food; pour what you need into a small bowl and discard leftovers that touched raw meat. Measure into a separate dish before you start rubbing, and you will keep the main jar clean for next time. These small habits protect guests and keep your blend fresh for months.""",
"hibachi-fried-rice-griddle":"""### Rice matters most

Fried rice is best made with cold, day-old rice. Fresh rice is too moist and turns mushy and sticky on the griddle. If you must use fresh rice, spread it on a sheet pan and refrigerate it uncovered for an hour or two to dry the surface. Break up clumps with your hands before it hits the steel. Spread it thin across the hot plate and let it sit undisturbed for a minute to toast, then toss. Season at the end with soy sauce around the edges, where it sizzles and caramelizes, rather than pouring it into the middle of the pile.""",
"backyard-cookout-game-plan":"""### After the party

The next morning, scrape and oil the grates if you did not get to it the night before. Pack leftovers into shallow containers so they chill fast, and use cooked meats within 3 to 4 days. Leftover grilled chicken becomes tacos, leftover steak becomes a steak salad or breakfast hash on the griddle, and grilled vegetables become a pasta sauce. Write a quick note about what ran out and what was left over, so next time you know exactly how much to buy.""",
"housewarming-and-wedding-gifts-for-cooks":"""### Registry etiquette for cooks

If you are building a registry, add a short note explaining your cooking style so guests understand why a grill or griddle is on the list. Mix price points so every guest can find something. And remember to send thank-you notes with a photo of the gift in use; a picture of the first cookout on the new grill is the best thank-you a giver can receive.""",
"charcoal-101-lump-briquettes-chimney":"""### Lighting without lighter fluid

A chimney starter makes lighter fluid unnecessary. Stuff two sheets of newspaper or a natural fire starter in the bottom, load the upper chamber with charcoal and light the paper underneath. In 15 to 20 minutes, the top coals will be ashed over. Pour them out and spread them. No chemical taste, no fuss.""",
"griddle-quesadillas-and-breakfast-tacos":"""### Keep them crisp

Serve quesadillas right away, cut into wedges with a pizza cutter. If you are making a big batch, hold finished ones on a wire rack in a warm oven rather than stacking them, which steams them soft. Brush the outside of each tortilla with a little butter or oil for a golden, blistered finish.""",
"perfect-smash-burgers-griddle":"""### Getting the smash right

Press each ball flat a single time, right away, while the meat is still cold and the griddle is very hot. Use a stiff spatula and press down hard with a second spatula or a heavy press on top for leverage. A square of parchment between the meat and the press prevents sticking. After that first press, do not press again; later pressing only squeezes out juice. Flip when the edges are deeply browned and lacy, add cheese and cook another 30 to 60 seconds.""",
"storing-a-flat-pack-grill":"""### A storage checklist

- Cool completely.
- Remove all ash.
- Scrape and wipe.
- Dry thoroughly.
- Oil lightly.
- Store dry and ventilated.
- Inspect at the start of the next season.""",
"tailgate-grilling-playbook":"""### Tailgate menu ideas by kickoff time

- **Morning games:** griddle breakfast sandwiches, breakfast tacos, sausage and pancakes.
- **Afternoon games:** smash burgers, brats with peppers and onions, wings.
- **Night games:** steak sandwiches, chili and cornbread, grilled sausages.""",
"holiday-gift-guide-for-cooks":"""### Wrapping a grill

Flat-packed grills are easy to wrap like a large book. Use sturdy kraft paper, tie with twine, and slip a recipe card and a sprig of evergreen under the knot.""",
"park-and-picnic-grilling":"""### What to bring

Pack a park bin with the grill, a chimney, charcoal, a lighter, tongs, a thermometer, gloves, a cutting board, trash bags and a water jug for dousing coals.""",
"smoke-flavor-wood-chunks-charcoal":"""### Chips vs. chunks

Small chips burn quickly on hot coals. Chunks smolder longer and give steadier smoke, so they suit open grills better.""",
"cast-iron-on-the-grill":"""### Recipes to try

Skillet potatoes, a seared steak basted with butter, shakshuka, skillet nachos and a fruit crisp all work beautifully in cast iron over coals.""",
"how-to-season-carbon-steel":"""### A seasoning schedule

New steel: three to five oven or fire layers before first use. Afterward: a thin oil wipe after each cook and a full re-season once or twice a season.""",
"griddle-cheesesteaks":"""### Variations

Try chicken cheesesteaks with sliced thighs, mushroom cheesesteaks with portobellos, or add hot cherry peppers for a spicy kick.""",
"make-ahead-cookout-sides":"""### Three sides that improve overnight

Vinegar slaw, marinated bean salad and quick pickled onions all taste better on day two.""",
"carbon-steel-vs-cast-iron-vs-stainless":"""### Quick verdict

Choose carbon steel for portable toughness, cast iron for heat storage and baking, and stainless for acidic cooking.""",
"brining-wet-vs-dry":"""### Bottom line

When in doubt, dry brine overnight. It is the simplest method with the biggest payoff.""",
"science-of-searing":"""### Remember

Dry surface, hot steel, space between pieces and patience before flipping.""",
"compound-butters-and-chimichurri":"""### Start simple

Begin with garlic-herb butter and classic chimichurri. Those two cover most grilled meals.""",
"spatchcock-chicken-charcoal":"""### Carving

Cut the bird into halves along the breastbone, then separate legs and wings. Slice the breast across the grain.""",
"meat-temperature-guide":"""### Key numbers

Poultry 165°F, ground meats 160°F, whole cuts of pork and beef 145°F with a 3-minute rest.""",
"homemade-bbq-sauces":"""### Start here

If you make only one sauce this year, make the sweet and tangy tomato style. It suits nearly everything.""",
"rust-rescue-carbon-steel":"""### Remember

Dry it, oil it and store it ventilated.""",
}
